ot to have taken part in the  attack itself  bernoulli his lawyers said that he was  uh  tortured or at least subjected to  enhanced interrogation  by the israeli security services and  there was a separate trial about the  admissibility  of some of his confessions some of which  were ruled out of court  as a result and there's also the  question of the wider issue  of settler violence the israeli security  services said that this was a landmark  case in tackling so-called jewish terror  but there has also been research done  into the  number of attacks that go unpunished uh  one  uh concluding that only eight percent of  people  uh who are suspected of involvement in  these sorts of attacks against  palestinian civilians  actually faced indictment and one  palestinian-israeli  member of the israeli knesset that i  just spoke to said that that was  part of the problem that this was an  exception that this kind of  verdict and this kind of sentence comes  down harry thanks very much harry  fawcett's uh reporting live there from  outside the courthouse in love let's  take it to  duma and al jazeera's correspondent  covering that end  of this developing story ibrahim have we  had a chance to gauge reaction  from the family yet  we went to the house where ahmed lives  now he is the 10 year old the only  survivor of the attack  he actually hasn't went to court today  and refused to attend  any of these sessions over the past few  years  he received the news when he was with  his  grandmothers both of these women each  one of them has lost a child  sad andre hammed both parents of  ahmad who were killed while they were  sleeping in this room alongside their  toddler  the 18 month old ali  the family wants to keep this house as a  museum is kind of a reminder of  settler attacks and actually one of the  grandmothers has told us that  let the settler rot in jail these were  were her words but they wanted us to  know  that nothing will ever compensate the  family  for their 